1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,344
A diver flips off a board.

2
00:00:02,344 --> 00:00:04,645
A thrown hammer spins in the air.

3
00:00:04,645 --> 00:00:07,085
A festival rocket bursts into pieces.

4
00:00:07,485 --> 00:00:08,527
Messy motion.

5
00:00:08,527 --> 00:00:13,053
Yet in each one, a single point
moves in a smooth, simple curve.

6
00:00:13,503 --> 00:00:16,721
Aarav watches a rocket climb
over the college ground.

7
00:00:16,721 --> 00:00:18,648
At the very top, it bursts.

8
00:00:18,998 --> 00:00:23,530
The pieces fly apart, yet one point
keeps the old curve, as if nothing

9
00:00:23,530 --> 00:00:24,430
happened.

10
00:00:24,281 --> 00:00:25,900
Which point, and why?

11
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,184
It is the centre of mass.

12
00:00:28,184 --> 00:00:29,766
Let's see how it moves.

13
00:00:30,366 --> 00:00:31,921
First, its velocity.

14
00:00:31,921 --> 00:00:35,569
Weight each velocity by its
mass, just like positions.

15
00:00:35,919 --> 00:00:39,546
V equals the sum of m
v, over the total mass.

16
00:00:39,546 --> 00:00:41,938
Acceleration works the same way.

17
00:00:42,338 --> 00:00:44,432
And here is the big result.

18
00:00:44,432 --> 00:00:49,164
Total mass times the acceleration of
the centre of mass equals the total

19
00:00:49,164 --> 00:00:50,307
outside force.

20
00:00:50,307 --> 00:00:52,337
Inside forces do not count.

21
00:00:52,937 --> 00:00:54,197
Our motion notebook.

22
00:00:54,197 --> 00:00:58,191
A rocket is launched at ten metres
per second across, and nineteen point

23
00:00:58,191 --> 00:00:59,091
six up.

24
00:00:59,176 --> 00:01:04,025
Gravity is the only outside force,
so it flies on a parabola, with its

25
00:01:04,025 --> 00:01:05,546
top at two seconds.

26
00:01:05,946 --> 00:01:08,828
At the top, it bursts
into two equal halves.

27
00:01:09,228 --> 00:01:12,039
One half stops dead,
and falls straight down.

28
00:01:12,039 --> 00:01:15,178
The other shoots forward at
twenty metres per second.

29
00:01:15,578 --> 00:01:19,232
Now watch the centre of mass,
midway between the halves.

30
00:01:19,232 --> 00:01:21,940
It keeps going, on the very same curve.

31
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:23,437
Slow-mo replay.

32
00:01:23,437 --> 00:01:24,825
Freeze at the burst.

33
00:01:25,175 --> 00:01:27,108
The halves push each other apart.

34
00:01:27,108 --> 00:01:29,871
Equal and opposite forces,
by Newton's third law.

35
00:01:30,271 --> 00:01:32,573
Inside the system, they cancel.

36
00:01:32,573 --> 00:01:36,456
The only outside force is
still the total weight, M g.

37
00:01:37,056 --> 00:01:40,465
Both halves land together,
at twenty and sixty metres.

38
00:01:40,465 --> 00:01:44,414
Their centre lands at forty,
right where the rocket would have.

39
00:01:44,914 --> 00:01:45,814
Quick check.

40
00:01:45,801 --> 00:01:48,855
Suppose one piece hits the
ground before the other.

41
00:01:48,855 --> 00:01:52,475
Does the centre of mass still
follow the parabola after that?

42
00:01:52,475 --> 00:01:53,645
Pause and decide.

43
00:01:56,745 --> 00:01:57,645
No!

44
00:01:57,177 --> 00:01:59,058
The ground pushes that piece.

45
00:01:59,058 --> 00:02:03,617
That is a new outside force, so the
centre of mass leaves the old curve.

46
00:02:04,217 --> 00:02:06,593
Now the proof, one step at a time.

47
00:02:06,943 --> 00:02:07,890
Step one.

48
00:02:07,890 --> 00:02:12,890
By definition, MX equals the
sum of m x, over every particle.

49
00:02:13,640 --> 00:02:14,540
Step two.

50
00:02:14,519 --> 00:02:16,835
See how fast each side changes.

51
00:02:16,835 --> 00:02:19,012
MV equals the sum of m v.

52
00:02:19,012 --> 00:02:21,521
Then MA equals the sum of m a.

53
00:02:22,271 --> 00:02:23,267
Step three.

54
00:02:23,267 --> 00:02:28,571
For each particle, m a is the total
force on it: outside forces plus inside

55
00:02:28,571 --> 00:02:29,471
forces.

56
00:02:30,035 --> 00:02:30,935
Step four.

57
00:02:30,902 --> 00:02:35,046
Inside forces come in equal and
opposite pairs, so they cancel.

58
00:02:35,046 --> 00:02:37,456
MA equals F outside, in newtons.

59
00:02:38,206 --> 00:02:39,236
Remember this.

60
00:02:39,236 --> 00:02:43,614
The centre of mass moves as if all
the mass were there, pushed only by

61
00:02:43,614 --> 00:02:44,692
outside forces.

62
00:02:45,392 --> 00:02:47,426
Why does the burst not matter?

63
00:02:47,426 --> 00:02:48,467
Link by link.

64
00:02:48,817 --> 00:02:52,083
The burst is just the pieces
pushing on each other.

65
00:02:52,083 --> 00:02:53,802
Those forces are internal.

66
00:02:54,202 --> 00:02:57,232
Every push has an equal
and opposite partner.

67
00:02:57,632 --> 00:02:59,354
So they add up to zero.

68
00:02:59,354 --> 00:03:04,235
Only gravity acts on the whole system,
and the centre stays on its parabola.

69
00:03:04,835 --> 00:03:06,002
Side by side.

70
00:03:06,002 --> 00:03:09,663
No burst: the rocket itself
lands at forty metres.

71
00:03:10,063 --> 00:03:14,770
A burst: the pieces scatter, but their
centre of mass still lands at forty.

72
00:03:15,370 --> 00:03:16,294
Now you try.

73
00:03:16,294 --> 00:03:19,947
Aarav walks along a boat on
still water, with no water drag.

74
00:03:19,947 --> 00:03:23,052
Does the centre of mass of
Aarav and the boat move?

75
00:03:23,052 --> 00:03:24,200
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:28,300
No!

77
00:03:27,843 --> 00:03:30,576
His feet and the boat push on each other.

78
00:03:30,576 --> 00:03:32,299
Those forces are internal.

79
00:03:32,299 --> 00:03:35,321
So as he walks forward,
the boat slides back.

80
00:03:35,971 --> 00:03:37,661
Now, one worked example.

81
00:03:38,011 --> 00:03:42,343
Blocks of two and three kilograms
sit on smooth ice, a squeezed spring

82
00:03:42,343 --> 00:03:43,340
between them.

83
00:03:43,340 --> 00:03:46,567
A ten newton push acts on
the two kilogram block.

84
00:03:46,567 --> 00:03:48,973
Find the centre of mass acceleration.

85
00:03:49,473 --> 00:03:51,917
Only the push is an outside force.

86
00:03:51,917 --> 00:03:55,423
The spring is inside the
system, so it drops out.

87
00:03:56,173 --> 00:03:58,887
A equals F outside over the total mass.

88
00:03:58,887 --> 00:04:00,031
Ten over five.

89
00:04:00,031 --> 00:04:02,107
Two metres per second squared.

90
00:04:02,857 --> 00:04:07,102
At one moment, the two kilogram block
accelerates at three point five.

91
00:04:07,102 --> 00:04:08,868
What about the other block?

92
00:04:09,618 --> 00:04:12,694
MA equals m one a one plus m two a two.

93
00:04:12,694 --> 00:04:15,149
Ten equals seven plus three a two.

94
00:04:15,149 --> 00:04:18,091
So a two is one metre per second squared.

95
00:04:18,741 --> 00:04:20,205
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:20,555 --> 00:04:24,147
Many students think a burst knocks
the centre of mass off its curve.

97
00:04:24,547 --> 00:04:26,303
But a burst is internal.

98
00:04:26,303 --> 00:04:30,899
Its forces cancel, so the centre
keeps its parabola, until a piece hits

99
00:04:30,899 --> 00:04:31,822
the ground.

100
00:04:32,472 --> 00:04:33,876
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:33,876 --> 00:04:38,438
In shells that burst in flight, and
in people walking on boats or planks.

102
00:04:39,038 --> 00:04:40,188
Exam level now.

103
00:04:40,188 --> 00:04:43,499
A three kilogram shell has
a range of sixty metres.

104
00:04:43,499 --> 00:04:47,308
At the top, it splits into two
kilograms and one kilogram.

105
00:04:47,308 --> 00:04:50,055
The two kilogram piece
falls straight down.

106
00:04:50,055 --> 00:04:52,607
Where does the one kilogram piece land?

107
00:04:52,607 --> 00:04:53,805
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:59,205 --> 00:05:00,605
The answer is B.

109
00:05:00,605 --> 00:05:02,829
One hundred and twenty metres.

110
00:05:03,329 --> 00:05:08,353
Both pieces land together, so the centre
of mass still lands at sixty metres.

111
00:05:08,853 --> 00:05:13,200
The two kilogram piece lands
below the top, at thirty metres.

112
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:18,122
Sixty equals two times thirty,
plus one times x, over three.

113
00:05:18,122 --> 00:05:20,952
So x is one hundred and twenty metres.

114
00:05:21,552 --> 00:05:23,093
Here is your recap.

115
00:05:23,093 --> 00:05:24,677
Screenshot this card.

116
00:05:27,877 --> 00:05:29,816
Can you answer these without help?

117
00:05:30,216 --> 00:05:31,116
One.

118
00:05:30,698 --> 00:05:34,547
Why can inside forces not change
how the centre of mass moves?

119
00:05:35,447 --> 00:05:36,347
Two.

120
00:05:35,894 --> 00:05:37,569
A shell bursts at its top.

121
00:05:37,569 --> 00:05:40,006
What path does its centre of mass follow?

122
00:05:40,906 --> 00:05:41,806
Three.

123
00:05:41,399 --> 00:05:43,976
When does the centre of
mass leave the parabola?

124
00:05:44,876 --> 00:05:48,647
Next time: two skaters push
apart from rest and glide off.

125
00:05:48,647 --> 00:05:50,971
Yet their total momentum stays zero.

126
00:05:50,971 --> 00:05:53,594
Linear momentum of a system of particles.
